course: history
lesson: 52
title: China: the First Emperor, the Terracotta Army and the Great Wall
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Mei says the First Emperor was the greatest ruler ever; the teacher says he was a tyrant. In 221 BC Qin Shi Huang united China and made writing, coins, weights and even the width of cart axles the same everywhere, but he also used forced labour, burned books and punished harshly. He was both a unifier and a tyrant.
explain: In 1974 farmers digging a well near Xi'an hit clay: they had found the Terracotta Army, thousands of life-size soldiers guarding his tomb, each face different. The tomb itself is still closed, to protect it.
explain: Mei also says the Great Wall is one wall you can see from space. Imagine a hair stretched across a football pitch: very long, too thin to see from far away. The Wall is many walls from many dynasties, and even China's astronaut Yang Liwei said he could not see it from orbit.
explain: After the Qin came the Han, a dynasty so important that most Chinese people still call themselves Han; they chose officials by exams, sent the traveller Zhang Qian west, and improved paper.
fact: Qin Shi Huang united China in 221 BC and standardised writing, coins, weights and the width of cart axles, and built roads.
fact: The First Emperor was also very harsh: forced labour, the burning of books and cruel punishments are recorded, and his dynasty fell soon after he died.
fact: The Terracotta Army near Xi'an was found in 1974 by farmers digging a well; its thousands of life-size clay soldiers have different faces and hairstyles, made from shared moulds with details added by hand.
fact: The historian Sima Qian wrote that the emperor's tomb has rivers of mercury, a poisonous liquid metal; soil tests show high mercury levels, and archaeologists have not opened the main tomb, mainly to protect it.
fact: The Great Wall is very long but only a few metres wide, and astronauts, including China's Yang Liwei in 2003, said they could not see it with the naked eye (without a telescope) from orbit.
fact: A 2012 Chinese survey counted all the wall sections from different dynasties at about 21,196 km; most of the stone wall seen in photos was built under the Ming.
fact: The walls helped control borders and trade, but enemies did get past: the Mongols conquered China, and the Manchus, a people from the north-east, came through the Shanhaiguan pass, a gateway in the Wall, in 1644.
fact: The legend of Meng Jiangnu tells of a wife whose husband died building the wall, and whose weeping brought a section down; it shows how much the builders suffered.
fact: The Han dynasty, 206 BC–220 AD, was so important that the majority people of China call themselves Han, and Chinese characters are «Han characters»; China also has 55 officially recognised minorities.
fact: China's imperial exams, begun in early form under the Han and Sui and fully developed under the Tang and Song, chose officials by tests of learning, not by birth, until 1905, and they influenced Western civil service exams (tests for government office jobs).
fact: Zhang Qian travelled west for the Han emperor around 138–126 BC, was held prisoner by the Xiongnu, horsemen of the northern steppe, for about ten years, and brought back knowledge of Central Asia that helped open the Silk Road; that was more than 1,300 years before the Italian Marco Polo travelled east to China.
fact: Tradition says Cai Lun invented paper in 105 AD, but older Chinese paper from about 200 BC has been found, so he improved it rather than invented it.
exercise: In which year did Qin Shi Huang unite China? | 221 BC | 1066 AD | 1492 AD | 1206 AD
exercise: What did the First Emperor make the same everywhere? | Writing, coins, weights and cart axles | Everyone's clothes, hair and family names | The weather and the seasons | Only the colour of houses
exercise: Which harsh deeds of the First Emperor are recorded? | Forced labour, burning books and cruel punishments | Banning rice and tea from every table in the empire | Selling China to Rome | Closing every road
exercise: Who found the Terracotta Army in 1974? | Farmers digging a well | Astronauts looking down from orbit | Marco Polo on his travels | Roman soldiers on the Silk Road
exercise: How were the clay soldiers made with different faces? | Shared moulds with details added by hand | Each was a real soldier frozen solid in clay | They were printed by a machine | They were carved from one mountain
exercise: Why have archaeologists not opened the main tomb? | Mainly to protect it | Because of a proven curse | Because it is empty | Because it is under the sea
exercise: Which metal do soil tests near the tomb show a lot of? | Mercury | Gold dust | Tin and copper | Uranium ore
exercise: Why is the Great Wall hard to see from orbit? | It is long but only a few metres wide | It is painted blue, so it looks just like the sea | It is underground | It was knocked down
exercise: Which Chinese astronaut said he could not see the Wall from orbit? | Yang Liwei | Admiral Zheng He | General Sun Tzu | Cai Lun of the Han
exercise: About how long are all the wall sections together, by the 2012 survey? | About 21,196 km | About 21 km | About 2,000 km | About 210,000 km
exercise: Which dynasty built most of the stone wall seen in photos? | The Ming | The Qin, in 221 BC | The Shang kings | The legendary Xia
exercise: How did the Manchus enter China in 1644? | Through the Shanhaiguan pass | By submarine | Through a tunnel under the Wall | By plane
exercise: What does the legend of Meng Jiangnu show? | How much the wall-builders suffered | That walls are made of tears | That the whole Wall was built in a single day and night | That women built the whole Wall
exercise: What do most people in China call themselves, after a dynasty? | Han | Ming | Song | Zhou
exercise: Until what year were the imperial exams held? | 1905 | 1066 | 221 BC | 2005
exercise: For about how long was Zhang Qian a prisoner of the Xiongnu? | About ten years | About ten days | About a hundred years | He was never caught
exercise: What did Cai Lun really do with paper? | Improved it; older paper has been found | Invented it from nothing in 105 AD | Brought it from Egypt on camels along the Silk Road | Banned it
test: Mei: «the First Emperor was the greatest ever». Teacher: «a tyrant». What is wisest? | He was both a unifier and a harsh tyrant | Only a great hero | Only a cruel tyrant who did nothing useful at all | He never really lived
test: bro says every clay soldier is a copy of a real guy's face. proven? | Not proven; each face is different, which is the real wonder | Yes, each one has a name tag with the soldier's real name | Yes, they are real soldiers turned to clay | No, all the faces are the same
test: A video says the tomb is never opened because of a deadly curse. What is the real reason? | Careful preservation of what is inside | A curse proven by scientists | It has already been emptied | It is under a lake
test: crap, my poster says «Great Wall: the only building you can see from space». fix it? | It's a myth; it's too narrow to see by eye from orbit | It's true, but only in winter when snow lies on top of it | It's true, it glows at night | Change it to «visible from the Moon»
test: The teacher says the Qin built the Wall; Mei says the Ming. Who is right? | Both partly; different dynasties built different parts | Only the teacher | Only Mei | Neither; the Romans built it
test: Mei says no enemy ever got past the Great Wall. What would the teacher answer? | The Mongols conquered China and the Manchus came through in 1644 | She is right; nobody got past | Only the Romans got past, by sailing round the end of it into the sea | Only dragons got past
test: If coins and weights were different in every region, what would happen to trade? | It would be slower and more confusing | It would be faster | Nothing at all | Coins would disappear
test: A friend says Zhang Qian «discovered the West» and Marco Polo was later. What is fairer? | Zhang Qian went west to Central Asia much earlier; Marco Polo went east | Zhang Qian reached London, Paris and Rome long before any European came to China | Marco Polo was first to everything | Neither ever travelled
test: The teacher uses «Cai Lun invented paper in 105 AD»; Mei read about older finds. What did both learn? | Dates can change with new discoveries | Books are always wrong | Paper came from Rome | Old paper is fake
test: Why might Britain have copied China's idea of exams for officials? | To choose officials by learning, not only by birth | Because exams are fun | To build a wall | To sell more tea
test: Why do Chinese characters get called «Han characters»? | The Han dynasty was so long and important | Han means «brush» | They were invented in 1905 | A famous film used that name
exam: Identify the measures attributed to Qin Shi Huang subsequent to the unification of 221 BC. | The standardisation of script, coinage, weights and axle widths | The abolition of all written language | The establishment of an elected parliament with members from every province | The introduction of paper currency
exam: Select the response consistent with the testimony of astronauts concerning the Great Wall. | It is not discernible to the unaided eye from orbit, being too narrow | It is the sole structure visible from the Moon | It is visible from orbit solely at night | It is visible from Mars in fair weather
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the main tomb of the First Emperor remains unopened. | The preservation of its contents by archaeologists | A curse attested by scientific inquiry | Its contents having been removed in antiquity | Its submersion beneath the sea
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of Cai Lun? | He improved the manufacture of paper, older specimens having been recovered | He invented paper ex nihilo in the year 105 | He introduced papyrus from Egypt by way of the caravan routes of Central Asia | He prohibited the use of paper
